/* =====================================================================
   THE ELEVEN SEALS - data/bestiary.js
   The Book of Monsters: the real legend behind every creature.
   Monsters: 3 kills show its name and land, 15 kills its tale,
   30 kills its secret. Giants and Seal guardians: 1 kill shows all.
   Change BESTIARY_NEED to change the counts.
   ===================================================================== */
const BESTIARY_NEED = { name: 3, tale: 15, secret: 30 };
const BESTIARY = {
  // ---------------- Hrimvald (Norse and Scandinavian legends) ----------------
  draugr: { land: "Norse sagas (Iceland, Norway)",
    tale: "In the Icelandic sagas, a draugr is a dead man who will not stay in his barrow. Greedy chieftains and cruel farmers came back as draugar, swollen and blue-black, heavy as an ox, to guard their treasure and torment the living. The saga of Grettir the Strong tells of Glámr, a shepherd who died on Christmas Eve and returned to ride the farmhouse roof at night, breaking the backs of men and beasts. Grettir wrestled him in the moonlight and won, but Glámr’s dead eyes cursed him with a terror of the dark for the rest of his life.",
    secret: "Old Norse people laid open scissors on the chest of the dead and carried the coffin out through a hole in the wall, so the dead could not find the door again. A draugr could only truly be stopped by cutting off its head, burning the body, and scattering the ashes into the sea." },
  troll: { land: "Scandinavian folklore (Norway, Sweden)",
    tale: "Trolls live in the mountains of Norway and Sweden: huge, slow, ugly and old as the rocks. Some have many heads, some carry their eyes in their hands, and all of them smell Christian blood from far away. In the famous tale of the Three Billy Goats Gruff, a troll under a bridge wants to eat each goat in turn, until the biggest goat butts him into the river. Many hills, stones and islands in Scandinavia are said to be trolls who stayed out too late.",
    secret: "Sunlight turns a troll to stone or makes it burst. Trolls also hate the sound of church bells and the smell of steel; a knife stuck in a doorway keeps them out of a house." },
  banshee: { land: "Irish folklore (the bean sídhe)",
    tale: "The banshee, the ‘woman of the fairy mound’, is an Irish spirit who wails when someone of an old family is about to die. Some saw her as a pale young girl, others as a washerwoman scrubbing the bloody clothes of the one who would die, still others as an old woman with long grey hair she combs with a silver comb. Her keening cry rises at night over the fields, and those who hear it know a death is coming in the house.",
    secret: "A banshee does not cause death, she only announces it. Never pick up a comb found lying on the road: it may be hers, and she will come to take it back." },
  mara: { land: "Germanic and Norse folklore",
    tale: "The mara is a spirit that sits on the chest of sleepers and rides them through the night, giving them bad dreams. Our word ‘nightmare’ comes from her name. In the Norse Ynglinga saga, King Vanlandi was trampled to death in his bed by a mara sent by a witch. People woke from her visits gasping, unable to move, and found their horses in the morning sweating, with their manes tangled into knots.",
    secret: "To keep the mara away, people slept with a knife or scissors under the pillow, put their shoes by the bed with the toes pointing out of the room, or hung a hag-stone (a stone with a natural hole) above the bed." },
  lindworm: { land: "Scandinavian and German folklore",
    tale: "The lindworm is a great serpent-dragon with only two legs or none, with a poisonous bite and no wings. In the Danish tale ‘Prince Lindworm’, a queen gives birth to a lindworm that eats every bride brought to it, until a clever shepherd girl wears ten shirts to her wedding. Each time the lindworm asks her to take one off, she asks it to shed a skin, until under the last skin stands a man.",
    secret: "Old tales say a lindworm grows from a small snake left to feed on gold. Lye, milk and brushes of birch twigs, used as in the Danish tale, can make it shed its skin and lose its power." },
  huldra: { land: "Norwegian and Swedish folklore",
    tale: "The huldra is a beautiful woman of the forest, with long golden hair and a lovely voice, who appears to charcoal burners and herdsmen alone in the woods. But behind her she hides a cow’s tail, and from the back she is hollow like a rotten tree. She can lure men away into the mountain, where they are never seen again, or bless a good man with luck in the hunt.",
    secret: "If a man marries a huldra in a church, she loses her tail and becomes human, though she keeps the strength to straighten a horseshoe with her bare hands. Never tell her you have seen her tail." },
  backahast: { land: "Swedish and Norwegian folklore",
    tale: "The bäckahäst, the ‘brook horse’, is a beautiful white horse that appears by rivers on foggy days. It looks gentle and asks to be ridden. But once someone climbs on its back, they cannot get off: the horse leaps into the water and drowns its rider. A Swedish story tells of children who climbed on one after another, the horse’s back growing longer for each child, until one little boy said ‘Look, Jesus’ cross!’ and the spell broke.",
    secret: "Saying the horse’s true name (Näcken, or Bäckahäst) out loud breaks its power. Some farmers even harnessed one with a magic bridle and made it plough their fields." },
  jotun: { land: "Norse mythology (Jötunheimr)",
    tale: "The jötnar are the ancient giants of Norse myth, older than the gods. The world itself was made from the body of the first giant, Ymir: his flesh became the earth, his blood the sea, his skull the sky. The frost giants live in Jötunheimr, beyond the edge of the world, and they are always at war with Thor and the gods of Asgard. At Ragnarök, the end of the world, they will march together to destroy everything.",
    secret: "Thor’s hammer Mjölnir is the giants’ great fear. But many jötnar are also wise: Odin himself went to the giant Vafþrúðnir to win a contest of riddles. Cleverness can beat a giant as well as strength." },
  hrimthurs: { land: "Norse mythology (the hrímþursar)",
    tale: "The hrímþursar, the rime-giants, were born from the frost of Niflheim. When the ice of the north met the fire of the south, the frost giant Ymir was born, and from his sweat came more giants. Odin and his brothers killed Ymir, and so much blood flowed that it drowned all the frost giants except one, Bergelmir, who escaped in a hollow tree trunk with his wife. All later frost giants come from them, and they never forgot.",
    secret: "Frost giants grow weak in warmth and spring. In the tales, the giant Hrungnir had a heart of stone with three sharp corners; Thor broke his whetstone and his skull with one throw of the hammer." },

  // ---------------- Volkmark (Slavic legends) ----------------
  vampire: { land: "Slavic folklore (Serbia, Russia, the Balkans)",
    tale: "Long before the novels, villagers in Serbia and the Balkans told of the vampir: a dead man who rose from his grave at night to drink the blood of the living. In 1725 a Serbian villager named Petar Blagojević was said to have come back after death and strangled nine neighbours, and Austrian officials dug up his body. They found it fresh, with new skin and nails, and blood around the mouth. The report spread across Europe and gave the world the word ‘vampire’.",
    secret: "Slavic villagers drove a stake of hawthorn or ash through the heart, put garlic and a sickle in the grave, or scattered poppy seeds on the road: a vampire must stop and count every seed until sunrise." },
  werewolf: { land: "Slavic and European folklore (the vlkodlak)",
    tale: "Across Europe, people believed some men could turn into wolves. In Slavic lands they were called vlkodlak or volkolak, ‘wolf-hair’. Some were born with a caul or a wolf’s birthmark; others were cursed by a witch, or put on a wolf’s belt to change shape. The old Russian epic tells of Prince Vseslav of Polotsk, a sorcerer who ran as a wolf at night from Kiev to the sea and back before the roosters crowed.",
    secret: "A werewolf turns back into a man if you call him by his Christian name, or if you wound him and draw blood. In later tales, only silver can kill one." },
  leshy: { land: "Slavic folklore (Russia, Belarus, Poland)",
    tale: "The leshy is the master of the forest. He can be as tall as the tallest tree or as small as a blade of grass, with a beard of moss and eyes that glow green. He leads travellers in circles until they are lost, imitates the voices of friends calling for help, and laughs and whistles in the dark. Hunters and herdsmen made deals with him, leaving bread and salt on a stump so he would let them come home safely.",
    secret: "If the leshy has made you lost, take off your clothes and put them back on inside out, and put your left shoe on your right foot. The trick confuses him and the path appears again." },
  rusalka: { land: "Slavic folklore (Russia, Ukraine)",
    tale: "A rusalka is the spirit of a young woman who drowned, often a girl betrayed in love. She lives in the river and comes out in early summer, during Rusalka Week, to sit on birch branches and comb her long green hair. She sings and dances in the moonlight and calls young men to dance with her. Those who join her are tickled or danced to death, or pulled down into the water.",
    secret: "Carry wormwood: if a rusalka asks what herb you hold, answer ‘wormwood’ and she will flee. During Rusalka Week, villagers did not swim and hung embroidered cloths in the birch trees as gifts." },
  zmey: { land: "Russian folk tales and byliny",
    tale: "Zmey Gorynych is the great dragon of Russian legend, with three heads (sometimes more), who breathes fire and lives in the mountains. He flies over villages to steal young women and princesses. In the bylina of Dobrynya Nikitich, the hero first beats the dragon while bathing in the Puchai river, foolishly spares him, and must fight him again three days long to free the prince’s niece Zabava.",
    secret: "When Dobrynya fought him, the dragon’s blood would not soak into the ground and nearly drowned the hero, until he begged Mother Earth to drink it. Cutting off a head is not enough: some heads grow back unless burned." },
  kikimora: { land: "Russian and Slavic folklore",
    tale: "The kikimora is a small, thin house spirit with a tiny head the size of a thimble and a body as thin as straw. She lives behind the stove or in the cellar. If the house is kept well, she helps with the chores and spins at night. But if the family is lazy, she breaks dishes, tangles the yarn, plucks the chickens and whistles in the dark. Some say if you see her spinning, someone in the house will soon die.",
    secret: "A tea of ferns used to wash the pots and dishes chases her away. Keep the house clean and tidy and she will leave you in peace." },
  vodyanoy: { land: "Slavic folklore (Russia, Czech lands)",
    tale: "The vodyanoy is the grandfather of the waters: an old man with a frog’s face, a long green beard and a body covered in scales and slime. He rules the river and the mill pond, rides on a giant catfish, and keeps the souls of drowned people as servants in his palace of crystal under the water. Millers and fishermen offered him gifts, because when he is angry he breaks dams and drowns people who swim after sunset.",
    secret: "Never swim after dark or on holy days. Millers buried a horse’s head under the mill or poured vodka and tobacco into the water to keep the vodyanoy happy." },
  koschei: { land: "Russian fairy tales",
    tale: "Koschei the Deathless is a terrible old sorcerer, thin as a skeleton, who kidnaps princesses and cannot be killed, because his death is not in his body. In the tale of Marya Morevna, Prince Ivan finds out the secret: Koschei’s death is in a needle, the needle is in an egg, the egg is in a duck, the duck is in a hare, the hare is in an iron chest, and the chest is buried under an oak tree on an island in the sea.",
    secret: "Find the needle and break it, and Koschei dies at once. Before that, every attack is useless, as many princes learned. Ivan needed the help of the animals he had spared to get it." },
  likho: { land: "Russian and Ukrainian folk tales",
    tale: "Likho One-Eye is Bad Luck walking on two legs: a huge, thin, one-eyed creature who lives in a dark hut in the forest. In the tale, a smith and a tailor go looking for ‘misfortune’, because they have never known any. They find Likho, who eats the tailor. The smith blinds her single eye with a hot iron and escapes, like Odysseus from the Cyclops, by hiding under a sheep. But Likho’s bad luck still cost him his hand.",
    secret: "The saying ‘Do not wake Likho while it sleeps quietly’ means: do not go looking for trouble. Once Likho has grabbed you, the only escape is to leave something of yourself behind." },
  // ---------------- Temurai (Mongol and Turkic steppe legends) ----------------
  almas: { land: "Mongolian folklore (the Altai and Gobi)",
    tale: "The almas is a wild human of the Mongolian mountains, covered in reddish hair, shy and strong, living like an animal in the Altai and the Gobi. Herders have told of them for centuries, and old Tibetan and Mongolian medical books even drew pictures of them among the animals. Travellers and explorers in the 20th century collected many reports of footprints and sightings, but no one has ever caught one.",
    secret: "Almases are said to fear fire and to avoid people. They are not hunted for meat: herders say harming one brings bad luck to the whole camp." },
  shulmus: { land: "Mongolian folklore (the shulmas)",
    tale: "Shulmas are demons of Mongolian legend that steal souls and bring sickness. They can take any shape: a beautiful woman, an old herder, a friend riding toward your yurt. In the great epic of Geser Khan, the hero fights many shulmas and demon kings who have stolen his wife and wrecked his land, and he defeats them with tricks as much as with his sword.",
    secret: "A shulmas in disguise often cannot hide everything: look at its shadow, its feet, or the way animals react to it. Shamans drive them off with drums, juniper smoke and the names of protective spirits." },
  albasty: { land: "Turkic folklore (Kazakh, Kyrgyz, Tatar)",
    tale: "The albasty is a female spirit of the steppe with long yellow hair and breasts so long she throws them over her shoulders. She attacks women who have just given birth and their newborn babies, sitting on their chests, stealing their breath, or taking their liver to the river to wash it. Across Central Asia, mothers and babies were guarded for forty days after a birth to keep her away.",
    secret: "Iron frightens the albasty: people kept a knife, a sickle or an iron pot by the mother’s bed, and fired guns or beat drums around the yurt. Pulling one of her hairs makes her your servant." },
  yelbeghen: { land: "Turkic and Mongol folklore (the yelbegen)",
    tale: "The yelbegen is a huge many-headed monster of Turkic epics, with seven or nine heads, who eats people and herds and rides a giant ox. In Kazakh and Altai tales, heroes and clever girls fight him or trick him. He is greedy and stupid, and his many heads often argue with one another, which gives the hero time to escape.",
    secret: "In many tales the yelbegen’s soul is hidden outside his body, often in a box or in an animal far away. Find it and destroy it, and the giant falls." },
  chotgor: { land: "Mongolian folklore (the chötgör)",
    tale: "A chötgör is a hungry ghost or evil spirit of Mongolian belief, often the soul of someone who died badly or far from home. It wanders at night, brings illness and bad dreams, and causes accidents to riders and herds. Some lurk near old graves and ruined camps; others follow a family from place to place.",
    secret: "Shamans and lamas chase chötgörs away with prayers, bells, drums and the smoke of juniper. Never call someone’s name loudly at night on the open steppe: a chötgör may answer." },
  mogoi: { land: "Mongolian folklore (mogoi means ‘snake’)",
    tale: "To Mongol herders, snakes are not just animals: some are spirits of the land and water, called lus and savdag, who own the springs and the hills. Kill a snake carelessly and the spirit of the place may send sickness, drought or a ruined herd. Great serpent spirits guard sacred mountains, and their anger is feared more than any wolf.",
    secret: "Leave offerings of milk at springs and sacred cairns (ovoo), and never dig, foul the water or kill snakes near them. A respected land spirit protects the herds." },
  mangus: { land: "Mongolian epic poetry (the mangas)",
    tale: "The mangas is the great many-headed demon of Mongol and Buryat epics, a cannibal giant with twelve, fifteen or even a hundred heads. He devours whole herds and peoples and steals the wives of heroes. The hero Geser Khan and many epic warriors spend years hunting mangas, who split their souls into animals and objects hidden around the world.",
    secret: "A mangas keeps its souls outside its body: in a fish, a bird, a golden box or a wasp. The hero must find and kill every soul, or the demon will rise again." },
  olgoi: { land: "Mongolian folklore (the Gobi desert)",
    tale: "The olgoi-khorkhoi, the ‘large intestine worm’, is said to live under the sands of the Gobi desert. It is dark red, as thick as a man’s arm and up to a metre and a half long. Nomads say it can spit acid that eats through metal and kill from a distance with a blast like lightning. It comes out in June and July, after rain, when the ground is wet. Many expeditions have searched for it; none has found it.",
    secret: "Herders say the death worm is drawn to the colour yellow and that it hates the vibrations of footsteps. Stay off the dunes after summer rain." },

  // ---------------- Shenlan (Chinese legends) ----------------
  jiangshi: { land: "Chinese folklore (the hopping vampire)",
    tale: "A jiangshi is a corpse so stiff it can only move by hopping with its arms stretched out. It rises when a soul fails to leave the body, often after a bad death far from home. In old China, Taoist priests were said to lead lines of corpses back to their home villages at night, ringing bells. The jiangshi drains the life energy (qi) of the living.",
    secret: "A jiangshi is blind and finds you by your breath: hold it and it passes by. A yellow paper talisman stuck on its forehead stops it; sticky rice, mirrors, peach-wood swords and a rooster’s crow drive it away." },
  huli: { land: "Chinese folklore (the fox spirit)",
    tale: "A huli jing is a fox who has lived so long it learned magic. After a thousand years it grows nine tails and can become a beautiful woman. In the classic tale ‘The Investiture of the Gods’, the fox spirit Daji possessed the queen of the last Shang king, led him into cruelty and ruin, and helped bring down the whole dynasty. But not all fox spirits are evil: many stories tell of loyal, loving foxes.",
    secret: "A fox spirit often cannot hide its tail when drunk or asleep, and dogs will always bark at it. An old mirror shows its true shape." },
  taotie: { land: "Ancient Chinese mythology",
    tale: "The taotie is one of the Four Fiends of ancient China: a monster of pure greed with a great face and a huge mouth, and in some stories no body at all because it ate even itself. Its face is carved on the ritual bronze cauldrons of the Shang and Zhou dynasties, more than three thousand years old, staring with bulging eyes. Its name became a word for gluttony.",
    secret: "Because it devours everything, the taotie was carved on cauldrons as a warning against greed, and some say as a guardian that eats evil spirits. Feed it nothing, and its hunger turns on itself." },
  gui: { land: "Chinese folklore (gui, ghosts)",
    tale: "In Chinese belief, the gui are the ghosts of the dead. Most rest peacefully when their families honour them, but those who died by violence, drowning or suicide, or who have no one to make offerings, become hungry ghosts with tiny mouths and huge bellies. During the seventh month, the Ghost Month, the gates of the underworld open and they wander the earth.",
    secret: "Families burn paper money, leave food and float lanterns on the water during the Ghost Festival so the hungry ghosts are fed and go back. Never whistle at night or turn around if someone calls your name." },
  shuigui: { land: "Chinese folklore (the water ghost)",
    tale: "A shuigui is the ghost of someone who drowned. It cannot move on to be reborn until it drowns someone else to take its place. It waits in rivers and wells, pulling at the legs of swimmers and pretending to be something floating in the water. Many old tales tell of a fisherman who befriends a water ghost and, when the ghost finally gets its chance, it lets the victim go out of pity, and is rewarded by heaven.",
    secret: "Don’t swim alone in strange water and never reach for floating clothes or toys. Peach wood and loud noise frighten a water ghost, and kindness can free it." },
  pixiu: { land: "Chinese mythology and feng shui",
    tale: "The pixiu is a winged lion-like beast with a single horn that eats gold, silver and jewels and has no bottom, so nothing ever leaves it. It was a guardian in ancient times, said to have fought for the Yellow Emperor. Today small pixiu statues are still kept in shops and homes in China to bring in wealth and keep it there.",
    secret: "A pixiu is loyal to its master and fierce to everyone else. It drives away evil spirits and bad luck, but it is said to hate the smell of dirt: keep it clean, or it will turn against you." },
  nian: { land: "Chinese New Year legend",
    tale: "Long ago, a beast called the Nian came out of the mountains or the sea every year at the start of spring to eat crops, animals and especially children. One year, an old beggar stayed in a village that everyone had fled. He covered the doors with red paper, lit fires and set off firecrackers. The Nian ran away in terror. That is why the Chinese New Year is still celebrated with red decorations, lanterns and firecrackers.",
    secret: "The Nian fears three things: the colour red, fire and loud noise. Red couplets on the doors, lanterns and firecrackers will drive it back for another year." },
  xiangliu: { land: "Chinese mythology (Classic of Mountains and Seas)",
    tale: "Xiangliu was a monstrous snake with nine human heads, a minister of the water god Gonggong. Wherever he went, the land turned to swamps of bitter poison where nothing could grow. During the great flood, the hero Yu the Great fought and killed him. His blood was so poisonous that the land where it fell could not be farmed, so Yu built a terrace for the gods there.",
    secret: "Xiangliu’s blood poisons the earth. Yu the Great beat him not just with strength but by controlling the water: drain the floods and the nine heads lose their power." },
  // ---------------- Yamakai (Japanese legends) ----------------
  oni: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "Oni are the great demons of Japan: huge, with red or blue skin, horns, tusks, tiger-skin loincloths and iron clubs. They live on lonely mountains and islands and in the underworld, where they punish sinners. In the famous tale, Momotarō, the Peach Boy, sails to Onigashima, the island of the oni, with a dog, a monkey and a pheasant, defeats the demons and brings their treasure home.",
    secret: "Every February at Setsubun, Japanese families throw roasted soybeans out of the door shouting ‘Oni wa soto! Fuku wa uchi!’ (‘Demons out! Luck in!’). Oni also hate holly leaves and the smell of grilled sardine heads." },
  kappa: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "The kappa is a river imp the size of a child, with a turtle shell, webbed hands, a beak and a dish-shaped hollow on top of its head filled with water. It pulls horses and children into rivers to drown them and loves cucumbers above all things. Yet kappa also keep their promises: many tales tell of a defeated kappa teaching a family the secret of setting broken bones.",
    secret: "Bow to a kappa. It is very polite and will bow back, spilling the water from the dish on its head, and without it the kappa loses all its strength. Families wrote their children’s names on cucumbers and threw them in the river as a gift." },
  tengu: { land: "Japanese folklore and Shugendō",
    tale: "Tengu are proud bird-men of the mountains. The oldest ones had a crow’s beak; later ones have red faces and very long noses. They are master swordsmen and wear the clothes of mountain monks. Legend says that the young hero Minamoto no Yoshitsune was taught swordsmanship by the king of the tengu on Mount Kurama, which is why he could not be beaten in battle.",
    secret: "Tengu punish proud and arrogant people, especially vain monks, and kidnap children who wander in the mountains. Humility protects you: they respect the humble and the hard-working." },
  yukionna: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "Yuki-onna, the Snow Woman, is a tall, beautiful woman with white skin, black hair and a white kimono who appears on snowy nights. In Lafcadio Hearn’s tale, she freezes an old woodcutter with her breath but spares the young apprentice Minokichi, if he never tells anyone. Years later he marries a lovely woman named Yuki and tells her the story, and she reveals herself and vanishes into the snow, sparing him only for their children.",
    secret: "Never speak of having met her, and never answer a voice calling in a snowstorm. Warmth and fire make her melt away." },
  nue: { land: "Japanese folklore (The Tale of the Heike)",
    tale: "The nue is a chimera with the face of a monkey, the body of a tanuki, the legs of a tiger and a snake for a tail. In 1153, a black cloud came over the imperial palace in Kyoto every night with an eerie cry, and the Emperor fell ill. The archer Minamoto no Yorimasa shot an arrow into the cloud, and the nue fell dead. Its body was put in a boat and floated away down the river.",
    secret: "The nue brings sickness and bad luck with its eerie birdlike cry. An arrow loosed by a brave archer and prayers to the gods drive it away." },
  nekomata: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "When a cat grows very old, its tail may split in two, and it becomes a nekomata. It can walk on two legs, speak, raise the dead like puppets with a wave of its paws, and eat people. For this reason old Japanese families sometimes cut the tails of kittens short. Mountain nekomata are said to be as big as a man, hunting in the forests.",
    secret: "A nekomata grows from a cat that is treated badly or left to grow too old. Kindness to cats is the best protection, and a dog will always sense one." },
  kamaitachi: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "Kamaitachi, the ‘sickle weasels’, ride whirlwinds in the mountains of Japan. They come in threes: the first knocks you down, the second slices your skin with claws like sickles, and the third puts medicine on the wound, so it doesn’t bleed or hurt. People walking in the wind would suddenly find deep cuts on their legs without knowing how.",
    secret: "Burn an old calendar and put the ashes on the wound, say the villagers of Niigata. The kamaitachi strike in cold winds: cover your legs when the whirlwind comes." },
  orochi: { land: "Japanese mythology (the Kojiki)",
    tale: "Yamata no Orochi was a giant serpent with eight heads and eight tails, so long it covered eight valleys and eight hills, with eyes red as cherries and trees growing on its back. Every year it ate one of the daughters of an old couple in Izumo. The storm god Susanoo promised to kill it. He had eight great vats of strong sake brewed; each head drank until it fell asleep, and he cut the serpent to pieces. Inside one tail he found the sword Kusanagi.",
    secret: "Strong sake: Orochi cannot resist it. Set out eight vats, one for each head, and strike when they sleep. Its tail hides a sacred sword." },
  gashadokuro: { land: "Japanese folklore (yōkai)",
    tale: "The gashadokuro is a skeleton fifteen times taller than a man, formed from the bones of people who died of hunger or in battle and were never buried. It wanders at night with a sound of rattling, ‘gasha gasha’, and bites off the heads of travellers to drink their blood. A famous print by Utagawa Kuniyoshi shows a giant skeleton summoned by the sorceress Takiyasha-hime crawling over a palace.",
    secret: "The gashadokuro is invisible until it attacks, but you can hear its bones ringing in your ears. Proper burial and prayers for the dead make it fall apart; Shinto charms keep it away." },

  // ---------------- Laut (Philippine and Southeast Asian legends) ----------------
  aswang: { land: "Philippine folklore",
    tale: "The aswang is the most feared creature of the Philippines: a shape-shifter that looks like an ordinary neighbour by day and becomes a dog, a pig or a giant bat at night to eat the dead and unborn children. It uses its long thin tongue to reach through roofs. Spanish priests wrote of the aswang in the 1500s, and the stories are still told in the province of Capiz.",
    secret: "Look into its eyes: your reflection will be upside down. Garlic, salt, holy water and a stingray’s tail whip keep it away, and a coconut oil lamp is said to boil when an aswang is near." },
  manananggal: { land: "Philippine folklore (Visayas)",
    tale: "The manananggal is a beautiful woman who at night separates her upper body from her legs, grows huge bat wings and flies off to hunt, leaving her lower half standing in the forest or the house. She lands on roofs and uses a long tongue to reach sleeping pregnant women. Her name comes from the word ‘tanggal’, to separate.",
    secret: "Find the standing lower half while she is away and sprinkle salt, crushed garlic or ashes on it. She cannot join her body again and will die when the sun rises." },
  tikbalang: { land: "Philippine folklore",
    tale: "The tikbalang is a tall, bony creature with the head and hooves of a horse and very long limbs, so that its knees reach above its head when it sits. It lives in bamboo groves and big trees and leads travellers astray: they walk in circles all night, unable to find the road home. When the sun shines while it rains, Filipinos say a tikbalang is getting married.",
    secret: "Wear your shirt inside out and ask politely for permission to pass, and the spell lifts. Pull one of the three golden hairs from its mane and the tikbalang must serve you." },
  kapre: { land: "Philippine folklore",
    tale: "The kapre is a huge dark giant who sits in the branches of big trees such as acacia, balete and mango, always smoking an enormous cigar. People see the glow of its cigar in the trees at night and smell the smoke. It is not always evil: it likes to play tricks, confuse travellers, and sometimes falls in love with a woman and follows her all her life.",
    secret: "A kapre holds a white stone. Steal it and it will grant your wishes. Never cut down a tree where a kapre lives without asking permission first." },
  bungisngis: { land: "Philippine folklore (Tagalog)",
    tale: "The bungisngis is a one-eyed giant from the mountains of the Philippines, always grinning with its big upper lip pulled back over its tusks. It is enormously strong but not very clever. In one tale, a carabao, a dog and a monkey outwit a bungisngis who keeps stealing their dinner, until the monkey catches the giant’s laughing lip in a tree.",
    secret: "The bungisngis cannot stop laughing; its huge lip often falls over its one eye and blinds it. Make it laugh, then run." },
  sigbin: { land: "Philippine folklore (Visayas)",
    tale: "The sigbin is a strange beast that walks backwards with its head down between its hind legs, like a hornless goat with long ears that it can clap like hands. It gives off a terrible smell and hunts at night, drinking the blood of victims through their shadow. Some families are said to keep sigbin in clay jars, and the creature brings them wealth in return for blood.",
    secret: "The sigbin is said to come out to hunt during Holy Week. Keep your shadow out of the dark corners of the house, and keep a light burning at night." },
  taniwha: { land: "Māori legends (Aotearoa / New Zealand)",
    tale: "Taniwha are great beings of the rivers, lakes, caves and the sea in Māori tradition, often like giant lizards, sharks or whales. Many are guardians (kaitiaki) of a tribe and its waters, warning of danger and saving people from drowning. Others are man-eaters: the hero Pitaka and his warriors killed the taniwha Hotupuku, who ate travellers on the road near Rotorua, and found the remains of many victims in its belly.",
    secret: "A guardian taniwha must be treated with respect, with offerings of a green branch and a prayer when passing its waters. The hunters of Hotupuku caught it with a huge rope noose at the mouth of its cave." },
  bakunawa: { land: "Philippine mythology (Visayas)",
    tale: "The bakunawa is a gigantic sea serpent with a mouth as big as a lake and gills like fire. Long ago there were seven moons in the sky, and the bakunawa rose from the sea and swallowed six of them. People feared it would eat the last one, so whenever there is an eclipse, they bang pots and pans and shout to make the bakunawa spit the moon back out.",
    secret: "The bakunawa hates noise. Beat gongs, pots and drums when it tries to swallow the moon, and it will spit it out and sink back into the sea." },
  // ---------------- Varnapur (Indian legends) ----------------
  vetala: { land: "Indian folklore (the Vetala Panchavimshati)",
    tale: "A vetala is a spirit that lives in corpses in cremation grounds, using them like puppets. In the famous story collection ‘Twenty-five Tales of the Vetala’, King Vikramaditya must carry a corpse hanging in a tree back to a sorcerer. Each time, the vetala inside tells him a story ending with a riddle. If the king knows the answer and stays silent, his head will burst; if he speaks, the corpse flies back to the tree. It happens twenty-four times.",
    secret: "A vetala respects wisdom. Vikramaditya won its friendship by his honesty, and it warned him that the sorcerer planned to sacrifice him. Mantras and fire keep vetalas away from the living." },
  pishacha: { land: "Hindu mythology",
    tale: "Pishachas are flesh-eating demons of Hindu texts, darker even than rakshasas, with bulging veins and red eyes. They haunt cremation grounds and empty places, eat the dead and possess the living, filling them with madness and strange illnesses. Some texts say they were created from the drops of the god Brahma’s anger, or born of the sage Kashyapa and Krodhavasa, the mother of wrath.",
    secret: "The pishachas fear fire, light and holy chants. People protected the sick with mantras and the light of lamps, and offered them food on a special night so they would leave in peace." },
  churel: { land: "Indian and Pakistani folklore",
    tale: "A churel is the ghost of a woman who died in childbirth or was badly treated by her family. She appears at crossroads and near graves as a beautiful young woman, luring young men away, but her feet are turned backwards. She takes her victims to her home in the wild, where they become old men in a single night or never come back.",
    secret: "Always look at a stranger’s feet: a churel’s point backwards. Families buried such women with iron nails and scattered mustard seeds on the road from the grave to keep her from finding the way home." },
  yali: { land: "South Indian temple art and legend",
    tale: "The yali is a guardian beast carved on the pillars of South Indian temples: a lion’s body with the trunk and tusks of an elephant, or the head of a horse, or a crocodile. It is stronger than an elephant or a lion, and sculptors show it with a stone ball that can roll inside its mouth but never come out. Yalis guard the doors of the gods and fight the forces of chaos.",
    secret: "A yali guards temples and sacred places. It is said to be invincible, but it serves the gods: respect their shrines and it will not attack." },
  naga: { land: "Hindu and Buddhist mythology",
    tale: "The nagas are a race of divine serpents, sometimes with many hooded heads, sometimes half human. They live in jewelled palaces under the earth and water and guard great treasures. The serpent king Vasuki was used as the rope to churn the cosmic ocean, and the naga Mucalinda spread his seven hoods over the Buddha to shelter him from a storm. But angry nagas bring drought and floods.",
    secret: "Nagas are the eternal enemies of Garuda, the great eagle; his image keeps them away. Honour them with milk and flowers at snake shrines on Nag Panchami and they will protect your water." },
  bhoot: { land: "Indian folklore (bhoot or bhūt)",
    tale: "A bhoot is the restless ghost of someone who died a violent or untimely death, or whose funeral rites were not done properly. Bhoots wander old banyan trees, empty houses and cremation grounds. They are said to cast no shadow, to speak through their noses, and to never touch the ground, because the earth is sacred.",
    secret: "Bhoots never touch the earth: lie on the ground and they cannot harm you. They fear the smell of burning turmeric, iron, and the names of the gods." },
  rakshasa: { land: "Hindu epics (the Ramayana)",
    tale: "Rakshasas are powerful demons of Hindu legend: shapeshifters and sorcerers who eat human flesh and disturb holy sacrifices. Their greatest king was Ravana of Lanka, with ten heads and twenty arms, who kidnapped Sita, the wife of Prince Rama. In the Ramayana, Rama raises an army with the monkey hero Hanuman, crosses the sea on a bridge of floating stones and defeats Ravana in a great battle.",
    secret: "Rakshasas are strongest at night and at dusk. Ravana could only be killed by a human, because he had asked to be safe from gods and demons and forgot men. Rama’s arrow struck where Ravana hid his nectar of immortality: his navel." },
  kumbhakarna: { land: "Hindu epics (the Ramayana)",
    tale: "Kumbhakarna was Ravana’s giant brother, so huge and hungry that he could eat the whole world. When the god Brahma offered him a wish, the goddess of speech twisted his tongue: instead of asking for ‘Indrasana’, the throne of Indra, he asked for ‘Nidrasana’, a bed of sleep. So he slept six months at a time. During the war in Lanka, he was woken with trumpets, drums and mountains of food to fight Rama’s army.",
    secret: "Kumbhakarna sleeps for months. It took a thousand elephants walking over him to wake him. Strike while he sleeps or before he has eaten, and his great strength fails." },

  // ---------------- Ndaru (African legends) ----------------
  tokoloshe: { land: "Zulu and Xhosa folklore (Southern Africa)",
    tale: "The tokoloshe is a small, hairy, mischievous creature of Southern African legend, called up by a sorcerer to cause trouble. It can become invisible by drinking water or holding a pebble in its mouth. At night it climbs into houses to frighten, bite and even strangle sleepers, and it is blamed for bad luck, illness and broken things.",
    secret: "Raise your bed on bricks: the tokoloshe is short and cannot reach you. Many families in South Africa still do it. A healer (inyanga) can banish it." },
  impundulu: { land: "Zulu, Xhosa and Pondo folklore",
    tale: "The impundulu, the ‘lightning bird’, is a black and white bird as big as a man that can call down thunder and lightning with its wings. It serves witches and is said to drink the blood of humans and animals. When lightning strikes the ground, people say the impundulu has laid an egg there that will bring bad luck to the village.",
    secret: "Healers dig up the lightning bird’s egg where lightning struck to destroy its power. Its fat and feathers are also prized by healers to make rain and medicine." },
  adze: { land: "Ewe folklore (Ghana and Togo)",
    tale: "The adze is a vampire spirit of the Ewe people that travels as a firefly. It slips into houses at night to drink the blood of sleeping people, especially children, and can make them fall sick and die. When caught, it turns into a human form, and it can possess people, making them into witches.",
    secret: "Close your windows at night and use mosquito nets: the adze flies in as a firefly. Catch it, and it must return to human shape. It loves coconut water and palm oil, which can lure it away." },
  kishi: { land: "Angolan folklore",
    tale: "The kishi is a demon with two faces: in front, the face of a handsome man, and on the back of his head, the face of a hyena with powerful jaws. He charms young women with his good looks and sweet words, then turns around when they are alone and devours them with his hidden mouth. He is said to come from the hills of Angola.",
    secret: "Never trust a charming stranger who keeps his hair long or covered and never turns his back to you: the hyena face is hidden there." },
  inkanyamba: { land: "Zulu folklore (KwaZulu-Natal)",
    tale: "The inkanyamba is a giant serpent with a horse-like head that lives in deep pools, especially below the Howick Falls in South Africa. It is the spirit of storms: when it flies from one pool to another in search of a mate, it brings violent summer storms, tornadoes and floods. Many people still say they have seen it in the waters of the falls.",
    secret: "The inkanyamba is drawn to shining tin roofs, which it mistakes for water. Healers perform rituals at its pools to calm it and end the storms." },
  eloko: { land: "Mongo folklore (Congo)",
    tale: "The biloko (singular eloko) are dwarf-like ancestor spirits of the deep Congo rainforest, with grass growing on their bodies instead of hair, long snouts and tiny bells. They guard the game and fruit of the forest and are angry with the living. They ring their bells to put hunters into a trance, then open their mouths wide enough to swallow a person whole.",
    secret: "Never go into the deep forest alone. Hunters wear charms and stay with their dogs; if you hear a little bell ringing among the trees, cover your ears and run." },
  ninkinanka: { land: "West African folklore (Gambia, Senegal)",
    tale: "The ninki nanka is a huge dragon of the swamps of West Africa, with a horse-like head, a crest of mirrors, and a long body. It lives in the mangroves of the Gambia River. To see it brings death: those who meet its gaze fall ill with fever and die within weeks. Some say the ninki nanka is a guardian spirit of the rivers and their riches.",
    secret: "Never look at its head directly. Elders say a healer can save someone who saw it, if they are reached quickly. Stay out of the mangroves where it hides." },
  grootslang: { land: "South African legend (the Richtersveld)",
    tale: "The grootslang, ‘great snake’, is said to be one of the first creatures, an elephant’s body with a serpent’s tail, so strong and clever that the gods split all its kind into elephants and snakes. One escaped and lives in a bottomless cave in the Richtersveld, known as the Wondergat, full of diamonds. It lures greedy people in with jewels and eats them. In 1917, the adventurer Peter Grayson went looking for it and was never seen again.",
    secret: "The grootslang loves gems. It will let you live if you offer it more precious stones than it already has; greed is how it catches its victims." },
  mokele: { land: "Congo Basin legend",
    tale: "Mokele-mbembe, ‘the one who stops the flow of rivers’, is a creature of the Congo River basin, said to be as big as an elephant with a long neck and tail. It lives in the swamps of the Likouala region, eats plants, and overturns canoes that come too close. Many expeditions have searched the swamps since the 1900s, with stories of tracks and sightings, but no proof.",
    secret: "Mokele-mbembe guards its river and attacks anything that enters, though it eats only plants. People say it feeds on molombo fruit, and fishermen fence off their waters with stakes to keep it away." },
  // ---------------- Azhar (Arabian legends) ----------------
  ghoul: { land: "Arabian folklore (the ghūl)",
    tale: "The ghoul is a desert demon of pre-Islamic Arabia, a kind of jinn that lives in lonely places and graveyards. It changes shape, often taking the form of a hyena or a beautiful woman, to lead travellers off the road into the empty desert, where it eats them. In the Thousand and One Nights, a wife named Amina is secretly a ghoul who eats rice with a needle by day and feasts on corpses in the cemetery at night.",
    secret: "A ghoul can be killed with a single blow: strike it a second time and it comes back to life. The old Arab poets said the ghoul’s feet are always those of a donkey." },
  roc: { land: "Arabian and Persian legend",
    tale: "The roc is a bird so huge it can carry off an elephant in its claws. In the Thousand and One Nights, Sinbad the Sailor finds a great white dome on an island, which turns out to be a roc’s egg. He ties himself to the bird’s leg with his turban to escape. On another voyage, his shipmates break open a roc’s egg and eat the chick, and the parents sink their ship with boulders.",
    secret: "Never touch a roc’s egg. The parents will hunt the thieves across the sea. Clever Sinbad used a sleeping roc as a ride off his island." },
  marid: { land: "Arabian folklore (the mārid)",
    tale: "The marid is the strongest and proudest kind of jinn, linked to the seas and to great waters. In the Thousand and One Nights, a poor fisherman finds a copper jar sealed with the seal of King Solomon. When he opens it, an enormous marid pours out in a column of smoke and swears to kill whoever freed him, since he waited so long. The fisherman tricks him back into the jar by asking how such a giant could fit inside.",
    secret: "A marid can be tricked by its own pride: dare it to prove it can fit inside a jar. Solomon’s seal can trap it, and it must grant wishes to whoever binds it." },
  sila: { land: "Arabian folklore (the si’lat)",
    tale: "The si’lat is a female jinn, cunning and shape-shifting, who lives among people in disguise. One old tale tells of a man who married a si’lat in Arabia and had children with her. She lived happily as a wife until one night she saw lightning over the lands of the jinn and flew away, never to return. The si’lat is the cleverest of the desert spirits, a sorceress who can bewitch a whole caravan.",
    secret: "A si’lat cannot hide from lightning over her homeland: it calls her back. Iron and the recitation of holy words break her spells." },
  nasnas: { land: "Arabian folklore",
    tale: "The nasnas is half of a person: one arm, one leg, one eye, half a head and half a body, hopping with great speed. The traveller al-Qazwini wrote of them in the 13th century, saying they lived in Yemen and Hadramawt and were hunted by the local people. Some stories say they are the children of a jinn and a human, others that they are people cursed by the gods.",
    secret: "A nasnas hops fast but has only one eye, on one side: approach from its blind side. Old writers said it hides in the palm groves." },
  qutrub: { land: "Arabian folklore",
    tale: "The qutrub is a creature of the graveyards and the night, like a werewolf or a male ghoul. It wanders tombs, eats the dead and attacks lonely travellers. Arab writers called it the male of the si’lat, and doctors in the Middle Ages even gave its name, ‘qutrub’, to a kind of madness where people wander tombs at night, howling.",
    secret: "Qutrubs avoid lights and crowds. Travel by day, with others, and never sleep in a graveyard." },
  ifrit: { land: "Islamic and Arabian folklore",
    tale: "The ifrit is a powerful and dangerous kind of jinn made of smokeless fire, living in ruins and the underground. In the Qur’an, an ifrit offers to bring King Solomon the throne of the Queen of Sheba before Solomon can rise from his seat. In the Thousand and One Nights, ifrits kidnap princesses on their wedding nights, imprison them in glass chests and turn men into apes.",
    secret: "An ifrit can be bound with Solomon’s seal or by a powerful sorcerer, and then must obey. Like all jinn, it is said to fear iron and the words of holy verses." },
  greatroc: { land: "Arabian, Persian and travellers’ tales",
    tale: "The great roc was so vast that its wings hid the sun. Marco Polo wrote in the 1290s that the people of Madagascar told of a giant bird that lifted elephants into the sky and dropped them to break them open. The Great Khan himself was said to have received a roc feather twelve paces long. Sinbad saw one rest on an island, and thought the sun had gone out.",
    secret: "The great roc is too big to dodge in the air: it strikes like a falling mountain. Hide in caves and gorges, where its wings cannot follow." },

  // ---------------- Thalassa (Greek legends) ----------------
  minotaur: { land: "Greek mythology (Crete)",
    tale: "The Minotaur had the body of a man and the head of a bull. It was the son of Queen Pasiphaë of Crete and a white bull sent by the sea god Poseidon. King Minos had the inventor Daedalus build the Labyrinth to imprison it, and every nine years Athens had to send seven youths and seven maidens to be eaten. The hero Theseus went in, killed the Minotaur, and found his way out with a ball of thread given by Princess Ariadne.",
    secret: "Take a thread into the maze and unroll it as you go, like Theseus. The Minotaur knows every corner of its Labyrinth, but the thread will lead you out." },
  harpy: { land: "Greek mythology",
    tale: "Harpies are winged women with the claws of birds of prey, called ‘the snatchers’. They were sent by Zeus to punish King Phineus of Thrace: every time he sat down to eat, the harpies swooped down to steal his food and foul the rest. When Jason and the Argonauts passed by, the winged brothers Zetes and Calais, sons of the North Wind, chased the harpies across the sea until the goddess Iris stopped them.",
    secret: "The sons of the North Wind beat the harpies with speed. Harpies avoid places protected by the gods, and their shrieks can be drowned out by bronze cymbals, as Heracles scared off the Stymphalian birds." },
  gorgon: { land: "Greek mythology",
    tale: "The Gorgons were three sisters with snakes for hair, golden wings and brass hands. One look at their faces turned anyone to stone. Two were immortal; only Medusa could die. The hero Perseus, helped by Athena and Hermes, flew to the edge of the world with winged sandals, looked only at Medusa’s reflection in his polished bronze shield, and cut off her head. From her blood sprang the winged horse Pegasus.",
    secret: "Never look a gorgon in the face: watch her only in a mirror or a polished shield. Even after death, a gorgon’s head can still turn enemies to stone." },
  satyr: { land: "Greek mythology",
    tale: "Satyrs are wild men of the woods with horse or goat ears, tails and sometimes goat legs, companions of Dionysus, the god of wine. They love music, dancing, wine and chasing nymphs, and they are lazy, greedy and cowardly. The satyr Marsyas found Athena’s flute and dared to challenge the god Apollo to a music contest; he lost, and Apollo flayed him alive for his pride.",
    secret: "Satyrs cannot resist wine and music. King Midas caught the wise old satyr Silenus by pouring wine into the spring he drank from." },
  lamia: { land: "Greek mythology",
    tale: "Lamia was a beautiful queen of Libya loved by Zeus. In her jealousy, the goddess Hera killed her children. Mad with grief, Lamia became a monster who stole and ate the children of others, and her face became terrible to see. Hera cursed her so she could never close her eyes, but Zeus gave her the power to take her eyes out to rest. Greek mothers used her name to frighten naughty children.",
    secret: "Lamia sleeps only when she takes her eyes out of her head. In later tales she charms young men; the philosopher Apollonius saw through one at a wedding feast, and her illusion of gold and servants vanished." },
  siren: { land: "Greek mythology (Homer’s Odyssey)",
    tale: "The Sirens were half-bird, half-woman creatures who lived on a rocky island surrounded by the bones of sailors. Their song was so beautiful that anyone who heard it forgot home and steered their ship onto the rocks. Odysseus wanted to hear it and live: he plugged his sailors’ ears with beeswax and had himself tied to the mast, ordering them not to untie him, whatever he said.",
    secret: "Wax in the ears, or a stronger song. When the Argonauts sailed past, Orpheus played his lyre so beautifully that the sailors did not hear the Sirens at all." },
  cyclops: { land: "Greek mythology (Homer’s Odyssey)",
    tale: "The Cyclopes are one-eyed giants. In Homer’s Odyssey, Odysseus and his men are trapped in the cave of Polyphemus, son of Poseidon, who eats two of them every meal. Odysseus tells him his name is ‘Nobody’, makes him drunk with strong wine, and blinds his single eye with a burning stake. When the other Cyclopes ask who hurt him, Polyphemus shouts ‘Nobody is killing me!’ and no one comes to help.",
    secret: "Strong wine puts a Cyclops to sleep, and his single eye is his weak point. Escape his cave clinging under his sheep: he counts them with his hands, but only feels their backs." },
  hydra: { land: "Greek mythology (Lerna)",
    tale: "The Lernaean Hydra was a giant water serpent with many heads, whose breath and blood were deadly poison. Killing it was the second of the twelve labours of Heracles. Each time he cut off a head, two more grew back. His nephew Iolaus helped by burning each neck with a torch so no new heads could grow. The last head was immortal, and Heracles buried it under a huge rock.",
    secret: "Fire stops the heads from growing back: burn each wound as soon as you cut. Heracles dipped his arrows in the Hydra’s poison, which made them deadly ever after." },
  // ---------------- Tlalqui (Mesoamerican and Latin American legends) ----------------
  cadejo: { land: "Central American folklore (Guatemala, El Salvador)",
    tale: "The cadejo is a great dog with glowing red eyes and hooves like a goat, that appears to people walking alone at night. There are two: the white cadejo, a guardian sent to protect travellers on the road, and the black cadejo, a demon that follows drunks and sinners to lead them to their death. People who walk home late often tell of a large silent dog that walked beside them until they reached their door.",
    secret: "The white cadejo protects you from the black one. Never run from the black cadejo and never insult it: pray and walk steadily, and it may lose interest." },
  ahuizotl: { land: "Aztec mythology",
    tale: "The ahuizotl is a water creature of Aztec legend, like a dog or an otter with smooth black fur, monkey-like hands, and an extra hand at the end of its tail. It lives in deep pools and lakes and grabs people at the water’s edge with its tail hand, pulling them under. Weeks later, their bodies float up without eyes, nails or teeth, which it likes to eat. Fray Bernardino de Sahagún recorded the legend in the 1500s.",
    secret: "The ahuizotl imitates a crying baby to lure people to the edge. Aztec priests said its victims belonged to the rain god Tlaloc and were buried with honour; stay away from the water when you hear a child crying." },
  llorona: { land: "Mexican and Latin American folklore",
    tale: "La Llorona, ‘the Weeping Woman’, is the ghost of a beautiful woman named María who drowned her own children in a river in a moment of rage and despair, after the man she loved left her. Overcome with grief, she drowned herself too. She was not allowed into the afterlife until she found her children, so she wanders the rivers at night in a white dress, crying ‘¡Ay, mis hijos!’ — ‘Oh, my children!’",
    secret: "If her cry sounds far away, she is close; if it sounds close, she is far. She may take children who wander by the water at night: stay home after dark. Prayers and a rosary keep her away." },
  chupacabra: { land: "Puerto Rican and Latin American legend",
    tale: "The chupacabra, ‘goat-sucker’, is a modern legend. It began in Puerto Rico in 1995, when farmers found goats, sheep and chickens dead, with small holes in their necks and their blood drained. Witnesses described a creature about a metre tall with grey-green scaly skin, big red eyes and spines down its back, which hopped like a kangaroo. Soon the sightings spread to Mexico, Chile and the United States.",
    secret: "The chupacabra hunts only at night and targets animals in pens. Strong fences, lights and dogs keep it away. Many ‘chupacabras’ that were caught turned out to be coyotes with mange." },
  sisimite: { land: "Central American folklore (Belize, Guatemala, Honduras)",
    tale: "The sisimite is a giant, hairy creature of the Central American mountains, stronger than ten men, with feet that point backwards so that anyone following its tracks walks the wrong way. It lives in caves and kidnaps women to take as wives. In some Maya tales, the sisimite tries to learn the secret of fire from humans so it can rule them.",
    secret: "Follow its tracks the opposite way, since its feet point backwards. It envies the secret of fire: keep a fire burning and it keeps its distance." },
  nahual: { land: "Mesoamerican folklore (Mexico, Guatemala)",
    tale: "A nahual is a person who can change into an animal: a jaguar, a coyote, an owl or a turkey. The belief goes back to the Aztecs and Maya, who said every person has an animal spirit twin. A nahual sorcerer uses this power to steal, to spy on enemies or to hurt people at night, then returns to human form before dawn.",
    secret: "Wound the animal, and the next morning the nahual will have the same wound in human form. Salt and the sign of the cross keep it from changing back if you find its hidden human skin." },
  xtabay: { land: "Maya folklore (Yucatán)",
    tale: "The xtabay is a demon woman of the Yucatán who appears under ceiba trees at night, combing her long black hair with the spines of a cactus. She lures men with her beauty and sweet voice deep into the forest, where she embraces them, turns into a thorny tree or a serpent, and they are found dead among the thorns. The legend says she grew from a cruel woman who pretended to be kind.",
    secret: "The xtabay lives in the tzacam cactus and the shadow of the ceiba tree. Never follow a beautiful stranger into the forest at night; men say her hair hides a snake’s body." },
  camazotz: { land: "Maya mythology (the Popol Vuh)",
    tale: "Camazotz, the ‘death bat’, is a giant vampire bat of the Maya underworld, Xibalba. In the Popol Vuh, the Hero Twins Hunahpu and Xbalanque must spend a night in the House of Bats. They hide inside their own blowguns to sleep safely. Before dawn, Hunahpu peeks out to see if the sun has risen, and Camazotz snatches off his head in a single swoop.",
    secret: "Stay hidden until the sun has truly risen; one careless peek is enough for Camazotz. The twins later won back Hunahpu’s head with a trick, using a squash in its place." },
  cipactli: { land: "Aztec mythology",
    tale: "Cipactli was a monstrous sea creature of the Aztec creation story, half crocodile, half fish and toad, with a mouth at every joint of its body, always hungry. The gods Quetzalcoatl and Tezcatlipoca turned into great serpents and pulled it apart: its head became the thirteen heavens, its tail the underworld, and its body the earth. Tezcatlipoca lost his foot in the fight, using it as bait.",
    secret: "Cipactli was beaten by two gods working together, one taking the bait while the other struck. Its endless hunger is its weakness: it bites at anything thrown in front of it." },
  tzitzimitl: { land: "Aztec mythology",
    tale: "The tzitzimimeh are star demons of Aztec belief: skeletal female deities with claws, made of the stars that appear during eclipses. They attack the sun. The Aztecs feared that if the sacred New Fire was not lit at the end of each 52-year calendar cycle, the tzitzimimeh would come down from the sky and devour all humans. During eclipses, pregnant women hid indoors.",
    secret: "Light the New Fire. The Aztecs kept watch every 52 years on the Hill of the Star; when the stars moved on and the fire was lit again, the star demons could not descend." },
  // ---------------- three more legends for each kingdom ----------------
  myling: { land: "Scandinavian folklore (Sweden, Norway, Finland)",
    tale: "A myling is the ghost of a child who died unbaptised or was abandoned in the forest by its mother. It cannot rest. At night it chases lonely travellers and jumps on their backs, begging to be carried to the churchyard so it can be buried in holy ground. With every step toward the church it grows heavier, as heavy as a stone, then as a horse. If the traveller cannot reach the churchyard in time, the myling kills them in its rage.",
    secret: "Carry it all the way to the churchyard and it will finally rest. Giving it a name, as a priest would at a baptism, is said to free it at once." },
  nokken: { land: "Norwegian and Swedish folklore (the Nøkk or Näcken)",
    tale: "Nøkken is a water spirit that lives in lakes and rivers. He can take any shape, but he loves to sit on a rock in the waterfall, a beautiful young man playing the fiddle so sweetly that anyone who hears him must dance until they drop or walk into the water. In the old paintings he is half hidden in the pond, only his eyes and his hair above the surface. Musicians sometimes went to him with a gift to learn his magic tunes.",
    secret: "Nøkken cannot stand steel: drop a knife or a needle into the water before you swim and say ‘Nyk, nyk, needle in the water’. Saying his name aloud also breaks his power." },
  kraken: { land: "Norwegian sea legends",
    tale: "The kraken is a sea monster so huge that sailors mistook its back for an island and landed on it. It lives off the coasts of Norway and Greenland. When it rises, the sea boils with fish, and fishermen hurried to catch them before the kraken sank again, because its going down made a whirlpool that could swallow ships. In 1752, Bishop Erik Pontoppidan described it in his natural history of Norway. Today some think the legend came from sightings of giant squid.",
    secret: "Fishermen watched their depth lines: if the sea suddenly became shallow where it should be deep, the kraken was rising beneath them, and they rowed away as fast as they could." },
  babayaga: { land: "Russian and Slavic fairy tales",
    tale: "Baba Yaga is the most famous witch of Slavic tales: a bony old woman with iron teeth who flies through the sky in a mortar, steering with a pestle and sweeping away her tracks with a broom. She lives deep in the forest in a hut that stands on chicken legs and turns to face whoever calls it. A fence of human bones with skulls for lanterns surrounds it. She eats children, yet she sometimes helps brave heroes who are polite to her.",
    secret: "Speak to the hut with the old words: ‘Little hut, turn your back to the forest and your front to me.’ Be polite, do her chores well, and in the tale of Vasilisa a little doll in your pocket can do the impossible tasks for you." },
  poludnitsa: { land: "Slavic folklore (Poland, Russia, Czech lands)",
    tale: "Poludnitsa, the Lady Midday, is a spirit of the summer fields, a tall woman in white who appears in the hottest hour of the day. She walks through the rye and stops people who work at noon. She asks them hard questions about farming or spinning, and if they cannot answer, or if they try to change the subject, she twists their necks or gives them a terrible pain in the head. Mothers warned children that she would steal them if they wandered into the fields.",
    secret: "Answer all her questions until the church bell rings one o’clock: then she must vanish. Resting in the shade at midday, as the old farmers did, keeps you out of her way." },
  bannik: { land: "Russian and Slavic folklore",
    tale: "The bannik is the spirit of the bathhouse (the banya), a little old man with long nails and wild hair who lives behind the stove. He likes to bathe after the people of the house, and every fourth steaming was left for him and the spirits. If someone came in at the wrong time, he would throw hot stones at them, pour boiling water on them, or try to skin them alive. Young girls also asked him about their future.",
    secret: "Leave him water, a little soap and a fir-branch broom after your bath, and never bathe after midnight. If he strokes your back with soft paws the future is good; with his claws, it is bad." },
  tepegoz: { land: "Oghuz Turkic epic (the Book of Dede Korkut)",
    tale: "Tepegöz, ‘eye on the top of the head’, is a one-eyed giant of the Turkic epic of Dede Korkut. He was born when a shepherd met a fairy by a spring. No arrow or sword could pierce his skin, and he ate the Oghuz people, demanding two men and five hundred sheep every day. The young hero Basat crept into his cave, heated a skewer in the fire and blinded his one eye, then escaped dressed in a ram’s skin.",
    secret: "His skin cannot be cut, and his ring makes him safe from all weapons. Only his eye can be hurt. After blinding him, Basat cut off his head with the giant’s own sword, the only blade that could cut him." },
  zilant: { land: "Tatar legend (Kazan)",
    tale: "The Zilant is a winged serpent of Tatar legend, with the legs of a rooster and the tail of a snake, that lived in the hills where the city of Kazan now stands. One story says the hills were so full of snakes that the people set fire to them all; the snake king, the Zilant, flew away but swore revenge. Another says it guarded treasure. Today the Zilant appears on the coat of arms of the city of Kazan.",
    secret: "Fire drove the serpents from the Kazan hills. In the old tales, the Zilant protects the city that bears its picture, and attacks only those who come to steal." },
  shurale: { land: "Tatar and Bashkir folklore",
    tale: "The shurale is a forest spirit with a horn on its forehead, long hairy fingers and a body covered in fur. It lures people deep into the woods and tickles them to death, laughing all the while. In the famous poem by Gabdulla Tukay, a woodcutter tricks a shurale: he tells it to put its fingers into a split log to help him, then pulls out the wedge, trapping it. The shurale screams for help all night, but no one comes.",
    secret: "Never tell a shurale your real name. The woodcutter in the poem said his name was ‘Last Year’, so when the other spirits asked who had trapped him, the shurale cried ‘Last Year!’ and they only laughed." },
  baigujing: { land: "Chinese classic novel (Journey to the West)",
    tale: "Baigujing, the White Bone Demon, was a skeleton who became a demon after a thousand years. In ‘Journey to the West’, she wants to eat the monk Tang Sanzang, because his flesh can make her immortal. She disguises herself three times: as a young girl, an old woman and an old man. Each time the Monkey King Sun Wukong sees through the trick and strikes her, but the monk believes he has killed innocent people and sends Monkey away.",
    secret: "Only eyes that see the truth can recognise her: Sun Wukong’s fiery golden eyes. Destroy her skeleton itself, or she will simply take another shape." },
  hundun: { land: "Ancient Chinese mythology (Zhuangzi, Classic of Mountains and Seas)",
    tale: "Hundun is Chaos itself: in some old books, a yellow sack-like creature with six legs and four wings, red as fire, with no face at all, which can still sing and dance. In the story of the philosopher Zhuangzi, the emperor Hundun was kind to his two friends, so they decided to give him eyes, ears, a nose and a mouth like everyone else, drilling one hole each day. On the seventh day, Hundun died.",
    secret: "Hundun is said to be friendly to the wicked and to butt the good. It is chaos without a face: it cannot see you coming, but it cannot be talked to either." },
  jiutouniao: { land: "Chinese folklore (the nine-headed bird)",
    tale: "The jiutou niao, or nine-headed bird, is a demon bird of old Chinese stories, also called the ghost car bird. It once had ten heads, but a hunting dog bit one off, and the wound still drips blood. Where a drop of its blood falls on a house, someone there will fall ill or misfortune will come. It flies at night, crying, and steals the souls of children.",
    secret: "Dogs are its enemy: when people heard the bird at night, they made their dogs bark and put out their lamps so it would pass by." },
  rokurokubi: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "A rokurokubi looks like an ordinary woman by day. But at night, while she sleeps, her neck stretches to an incredible length, and her head wanders through the house and the village, licking lamp oil and spying on people. Some do not know what they are. In a tale told by Lafcadio Hearn, a monk staying in a mountain hut finds the bodies of his hosts without heads; they are nukekubi, whose heads leave their bodies entirely.",
    secret: "If you find the body while the head is away, move it to another place. When the head returns and cannot find its body, it will bounce on the floor three times and die at sunrise." },
  jorogumo: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "A jorōgumo is a spider who has lived four hundred years and gained magic powers. She takes the form of a beautiful woman and invites young men into her house. She plays the biwa lute to charm them, then binds them in silk threads and eats them. The legend of the Jōren waterfall tells of a woodcutter who saw spider threads being wrapped around his leg while he rested; he tied the thread to a tree stump, and the stump was pulled into the water.",
    secret: "Tie her thread to something else, as the woodcutter did, and step away. A jorōgumo’s true shape appears in a mirror or in her shadow." },
  umibozu: { land: "Japanese folklore",
    tale: "Umibōzu, the ‘sea monk’, is a giant black spirit that rises from calm seas at night, with a round shaved head like a Buddhist monk and huge glowing eyes. It towers over the boats of fishermen and asks for a barrel. It will fill the barrel with seawater and pour it into the boat until the boat sinks. Anyone who speaks to it may be dragged under. Some said umibōzu were the spirits of drowned priests.",
    secret: "Give it a barrel with no bottom. It will scoop water forever and never fill your boat, and you can sail away while it is busy." },
  tiyanak: { land: "Philippine folklore",
    tale: "A tiyanak is a demon that looks like a crying baby, lost in the forest. When a kind traveller picks it up to help, it changes into its true form, a small creature with sharp claws and teeth, and attacks. Some stories say tiyanak are the spirits of babies who died before they were baptised. They lead travellers astray in the jungle and laugh at them.",
    secret: "Turn your clothes inside out, and the tiyanak gets confused and leaves you, laughing. Never pick up a baby crying alone in the forest at night." },
  pontianak: { land: "Malay and Indonesian folklore",
    tale: "The pontianak is the ghost of a woman who died in childbirth. She appears as a beautiful woman with long black hair in a white dress, and the smell of frangipani flowers announces her, followed by the smell of rotting flesh. When her cry sounds faint, she is near; when it sounds loud, she is far away. She preys on men and pregnant women, tearing at their bellies with long nails.",
    secret: "Drive a nail into the hole at the back of her neck and she becomes a beautiful, gentle woman again, as long as the nail stays in. Pull it out and she becomes a pontianak once more." },
  siyokoy: { land: "Philippine folklore",
    tale: "The siyokoy is a sea creature of the Philippines, like a merman with scaly brown-green skin, webbed hands and feet, gills and fins. It lives in the sea and the deep rivers and drags fishermen and swimmers down to drown them. Some say the siyokoy are the male cousins of the beautiful sirena mermaids, but much uglier and crueller.",
    secret: "Fishermen do not swim alone in deep water, and many carry an anting-anting, a protective amulet, when they go out to sea." },
  brahmarakshasa: { land: "Indian folklore and Hindu texts",
    tale: "A brahmarakshasa is the spirit of a learned brahmin, a priest and scholar, who misused his knowledge or lived an evil life. After death he becomes a rakshasa demon who still remembers all the holy texts. He is terrifying to meet, with two horns and a tail of fire, and eats people. But because he is so wise, some stories tell of students who dared to ask him for lessons, and he taught them in exchange for his freedom.",
    secret: "A brahmarakshasa is freed when he finally passes on his knowledge. Answer his questions wisely, or let him teach you, and he may leave in peace." },
  makara: { land: "Hindu and Buddhist mythology",
    tale: "The makara is a sea creature of Indian legend, part crocodile, part fish, often with the trunk of an elephant. It is the mount of Ganga, the river goddess, and of Varuna, the god of the waters. Makaras are carved at temple doors and gateways in India and Southeast Asia, their open mouths spitting out flowers and vines. In stories they guard the rivers and seize those who disrespect them.",
    secret: "Makaras serve the gods of water. Throw an offering into the river before crossing, and the makara may let you pass." },
  dakini: { land: "Hindu and Tibetan tradition",
    tale: "In old Hindu stories, dakinis are fierce female spirits who serve the dark goddess Kali, flying through the sky, dancing in cremation grounds and eating human flesh. Later, in Tibetan Buddhism, the dakini became the ‘sky dancer’, a powerful and wild female spirit who carries wisdom to those brave enough to face her. Both sides of her are frightening and both are strong.",
    secret: "A dakini respects courage. In the tales, the brave who face her without fear may earn her teaching instead of her anger." },
  asanbosam: { land: "Akan and Ashanti folklore (Ghana, Côte d’Ivoire)",
    tale: "The asanbosam is a forest vampire with iron teeth and iron hooks for feet. It lives high in the trees of the deep forest, waiting for hunters to walk underneath. Then it drops its legs and hooks them up into the branches, where it drinks their blood. In one tale, two hunter brothers go out separately, and one is pulled up by an asanbosam, but his brother shoots off its legs with an arrow.",
    secret: "Hunters never walk alone under the great trees. Look up before you rest in the forest; the asanbosam’s legs hang down like vines." },
  popobawa: { land: "Swahili folklore (Zanzibar and Pemba)",
    tale: "The popobawa, the ‘bat-wing’, is a shape-shifting spirit of Zanzibar, a dwarf with one eye, small pointed ears and huge bat wings. It attacks people in their homes at night, appearing as a shadow and leaving behind the smell of sulphur. Waves of popobawa panic have swept Zanzibar and the Tanzanian coast several times since the 1960s, with whole families sleeping outdoors in groups.",
    secret: "People say the popobawa only attacks those who sleep alone, so families sleep together, outside, around a fire. Those who refuse to tell others about it are visited again." },
  mamiwata: { land: "West and Central African water spirit lore",
    tale: "Mami Wata, ‘Mother Water’, is a water spirit honoured and feared from Nigeria and Ghana to Congo. She is often shown as a beautiful woman with long hair, with a fish tail or with a great snake wrapped around her. She can bring wealth, beauty and healing, but she is jealous: she may pull swimmers and boats under the water, and those she favours may never marry, belonging only to her.",
    secret: "Mami Wata loves sweet things, perfume, mirrors and the colours red and white. Offerings by the water win her favour; broken promises to her bring storms and drowning." },
  dandan: { land: "Arabian tales (the Thousand and One Nights)",
    tale: "The dandan is a sea monster in the tale of Abdullah the Fisherman and Abdullah the Merman. It is so huge it can swallow a whale or a ship in one gulp, and the sea people live in terror of it. The merman tells his friend that there is one thing that kills the dandan: if it eats a human from the land, or even smells their fat, it dies at once. So the merman covers the fisherman in fish oil to hide his smell.",
    secret: "The dandan dies if it tastes a human from the dry land. Sea people protected themselves with the fat of the dandan’s liver, rubbed on their bodies, which kept all sea monsters away." },
  shiqq: { land: "Arabian folklore",
    tale: "The shiqq is a kind of jinn that is only half a being: one eye, one arm and one leg, as if a body had been split down the middle. Its name means ‘half’ or ‘split’. It hides in deserts and wild places and attacks travellers. The early Arab historians wrote that a famous diviner of ancient Yemen was himself called Shiqq because he had only half a body.",
    secret: "Like the nasnas, the shiqq hops and turns slowly. The old storytellers said its blind half is where to strike, and prayers recited aloud drive it off." },
  ghaddar: { land: "Arabian folklore (Yemen and Upper Egypt)",
    tale: "The ghaddar, whose name means ‘the treacherous one’, is a jinn described by the 13th-century writer al-Qazwini. It lives in the borderlands of Yemen and sometimes Upper Egypt. It lures people with its voice and its tricks into lonely places, then torments them or terrifies them so badly that they lose their minds. Victims who escape are said to be left with sores that never heal.",
    secret: "Never answer a voice calling your name in the empty desert. The ghaddar uses tricks, not strength: those who stay with their caravan are safe." },
  chimera: { land: "Greek mythology",
    tale: "The Chimera was a fire-breathing monster of Lycia, with the body and head of a lion, a goat’s head rising from its back, and a snake for a tail. It burned the land and ate the flocks. King Iobates sent the young hero Bellerophon to kill it, hoping he would die. But Bellerophon tamed the winged horse Pegasus, flew above the Chimera, and pushed a lump of lead on the end of his spear into its fiery mouth. The lead melted and choked it.",
    secret: "Attack from the air, where its three heads cannot reach you, and use its own fire against it: lead melted in its throat is what killed it." },
  scylla: { land: "Greek mythology (Homer’s Odyssey)",
    tale: "Scylla was a monster with six long necks, each with a head full of three rows of sharp teeth, and dogs’ heads around her waist. She lived in a cave in the cliffs of a narrow strait, opposite the whirlpool Charybdis. Sailors had to choose between them. Odysseus chose to pass close to Scylla, losing six men, one for each head, rather than risk losing his whole ship to the whirlpool. Some said she was once a beautiful nymph, changed by a witch’s jealousy.",
    secret: "No one can fight Scylla; you can only sail past her quickly. The goddess Circe told Odysseus to row hard and to call on Scylla’s mother, Crataeis, to keep her from striking twice." },
  empusa: { land: "Greek mythology",
    tale: "The empusa is a shape-shifting demon sent by the dark goddess Hecate. She has one leg of bronze and one of a donkey, and her hair is on fire. She takes the form of a beautiful woman, a dog or a cow, seduces young men and drinks their blood. In the life of the philosopher Apollonius, a young man was about to marry a lovely bride who was really an empusa, until Apollonius exposed her.",
    secret: "Insult an empusa loudly and she flees shrieking: that is how a traveller in Aristophanes’ play drives one off. Her bronze leg rings on stones, and gives her away." },
  mapinguari: { land: "Brazilian Amazon folklore",
    tale: "The mapinguari is a giant creature of the Amazon rainforest, covered in thick red fur, with backward-pointing feet and, some say, a single eye and a second mouth in its belly. It smells so terrible that hunters faint, and it screams like a man. Some scientists think the legend might remember the giant ground sloths that once lived in South America, but rubber tappers and hunters still tell stories of meeting one.",
    secret: "Its fur is like armour; only the mouth in its belly or the top of its head can be wounded, hunters say. It follows its own trail backwards, so walk the wrong way along its tracks to escape." },
  pishtaco: { land: "Andean folklore (Peru and Bolivia)",
    tale: "The pishtaco is a terrifying figure of the Andes: a tall, pale stranger who hunts lonely travellers on the mountain roads at night, kills them and steals their body fat, which he sells to make candles, church bells or machines. The legend began in colonial times and reflected the people’s fear of foreigners who came to take what was theirs. Even in modern times, rumours of pishtacos have caused panic in Peruvian villages.",
    secret: "Never walk the mountain roads alone at night, and be wary of a stranger who offers help on a lonely path." },
  tunche: { land: "Peruvian Amazon folklore",
    tale: "The tunche is a dangerous spirit of the Amazon forest that announces itself with a whistle. It is the soul of someone who died a bad death and cannot rest, wandering the paths at night. When you hear its whistle, if you whistle back or answer its call, it will come for you and you will fall sick or die. People in the Peruvian jungle still go silent when they hear a strange whistle in the dark.",
    secret: "Never whistle back. Stay silent, light tobacco, and stay with others until the whistling moves away." },
};
